  "summary": "The added CI workflows install and execute code from the repository root, potentially allowing a pull‑request author to inject arbitrary code that runs with the GITHUB_TOKEN’s permissions.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "path": "src/vibey_tools/gh/.github/workflows/branch-health.yml",
      "explanation": "The workflow installs the local `vibey-gh` package from the repository (`python -m pip install --quiet -e \"$self\"`) after only verifying that a `pyproject.toml` contains `name = \"vibey-gh\"`. If a PR submits a malicious `pyproject.toml` with that name, the workflow will execute that code with repo‑write privileges via `GH_TOKEN`. This is a data‑source–to‑execution path that can be abused, exposing the repo to arbitrary code execution.",
      "recommended_fix": "Pin the tooling to a specific commit SHA or a trusted release on PyPI, instead of installing from the repository. Alternatively, remove the ability to install from the PR tree and use a read‑only artifact."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "path": "src/vibey_tools/gh/.github/workflows/ruleset-drift.yml",
      "explanation": "The same insecure installation pattern appears here: `python -m pip install --quiet -e \"$self\"` after only checking the project’s name. This duplicates the risk of arbitrary code execution.",
      "recommended_fix": "Apply the same mitigation as above: always install a verified, versioned package from PyPI or a locked commit, rather than from the repo."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "path": "src/vibey_tools/gh/.github/workflows/skip-markers.yml",
      "explanation": "The skip‑markers workflow uses identical logic to install `vibey-gh` from the repository root, exposing the same vulnerability to malicious PR content.",
      "recommended_fix": "Adopt a version‑locked or PyPI‑based installation rather than a local source install."
    }
  ]
}

